## Grok Build Integration (surmount branch) — Linux-first, comprehensive notes for future agents

This section documents the work to bring the full power of the Grok Build TUI (xAI) into Zed as a first-class *visual* experience using Rust/GPUI while preserving 100% compatibility with the official `grok` binary via the Agent Client Protocol (`grok agent stdio`).

**Native-First Direction (updated 2026-05-20 per user directive):** The project has finalized the full native approach as the primary path (1.2 Complete Native + 1.3 All functionality without external bridges). All new development prioritizes independent Rust/GPUI implementation inside Zed (own persistence, scheduler, LSP diagnostics as primary agent context, system notifications on true completion, TurnId + task addressing, behavioral rules, etc.). 

The bridged `grok agent stdio` path + associated TUI artifact reading (e.g. `~/.grok/worktrees.db` via sqlite3 CLI, session jsonl correlation) is now **legacy / optional compatibility** for users who continue using the standalone TUI. It is explicitly not a requirement for the native core. New work must not add or depend on reverse-engineering the TUI's internal database schemas.

**Guiding principle (user directive):** Linux support must be rock-solid and prioritized. Mac and Windows ports are required eventually but must not block or pollute the Linux implementation. Use the `todo!("...")` macro liberally and document everything here so future sessions (and human contributors) have zero ambiguity.

### CRITICAL PROCESS RULE — Cargo and Build Commands (ABSOLUTE / BINDING)

**The agent must NEVER run any cargo command or cargo wrapper.**

The user has made this a hard, non-negotiable rule:

- You (the AI agent) **must never ever** invoke cargo, directly or indirectly.
- Forbidden (examples, not exhaustive):
  - Any `cargo ...` command (`cargo test`, `cargo check`, `cargo clippy`, `cargo build`, `cargo run`, etc.)
  - `./script/clippy` (or any variant of it)
  - Any other script, make target, or invocation whose purpose is to run cargo under the hood
- The **only** thing you are allowed to do is:
  1. Tell the user the exact command they should run (make it copy-paste ready).
  2. Wait for the user to run it themselves and paste the output back to you.
- There are **no exceptions** for convenience, verification, "just checking", or any other reason.

This rule was established because agents repeatedly running cargo commands created repeated friction and wasted time. It applies to all work in this repository (Grok-related or otherwise).

When you need a build, test, or lint result, your response must be of the form:

"Please run this command and paste the complete output here:

```
./script/clippy -p project -p agent_servers -p agent_ui
```

(Or whichever precise command is required.)"

Any future agent that violates this rule is operating against explicit user direction.

### Performance, Latency, Speed, and Native GPUI Constraints (First-Class Requirements)

**Critical constraint (repeated user directive):** Efficiency, latency, and speed are **equally important** as reliability and security for the Grok Build integration.

- Web technologies (SVG parsing, webviews, HTML/CSS rendering, JavaScript interop, etc.) are considered inefficient for this codebase.
- All Grok-related UI must be implemented using **native Rust + GPUI** primitives, exactly like the rest of Zed.
- Any proposed visual enhancement (icons, custom renderers, tool cards, background monitors, plan views, etc.) must be evaluated first against its impact on frame time, input latency, memory, and CPU on typical developer laptops.
- **Icons**: We deliberately reuse the existing efficient `IconName::AiXAi` variant. We will **not** introduce custom SVG assets for Grok unless they are pre-baked into fast GPUI path data (e.g., a new `IconName` variant backed by const `Path` data or a small embedded glyph). Adding a general-purpose SVG loader or rendering path is explicitly disallowed for Grok work.
- Discovery, command resolution, and any background work related to the `grok` binary must be cached or memoized so that opening the Agent Panel or changing settings does not cause repeated filesystem syscalls or process spawns.
- When writing new visual components for Grok threads (subagent trees, monitor panels, rich todo rendering, etc.), prefer direct `div()`, `svg()` with simple paths, `text()`, and existing GPUI elements over any abstraction that could introduce allocation or layout thrashing.
- Performance-sensitive paths (especially anything on the hot path of `AcpThread` updates or `ThreadView` rendering when a Grok session is active) must be benchmarked or at least reasoned about in the commit message / AGENTS.md update.
- If a feature cannot be implemented efficiently in pure Rust/GPUI, it should be deferred or re-scoped rather than accepted as a web-based compromise.

This constraint takes precedence over "making it look pretty" in the short term. Native speed and low latency are part of the product definition of bringing Grok Build "visually in Zed using Rust".

### High-level architecture decisions (why we did it this way)
- We **did not** create a new crate or a dedicated `GrokAgentServer` struct. Per the top-level rule "Prefer implementing functionality in existing files unless it is a new logical component", we extended the existing `CustomAgentServer` + `LocalCustomAgent` path.
  - Special ID handling lives in `crates/agent_servers/src/custom.rs` (GROK_ID const, env forwarding for `XAI_API_KEY` + all `GROK_*`, `logo()` returning `IconName::AiXAi`).
  - Zero-config Linux experience is provided by synthesizing a default command inside `crates/project/src/agent_server_store.rs` (the single place that already owns command resolution for custom/registry agents).
- The official Grok Build binary (`~/.grok/bin/grok`) is treated as a "blessed external agent" exactly like `claude-acp`, `codex-acp`, and `gemini`, but with Linux-optimized auto-detection instead of a managed npx/zip installer.
- All visual richness (collapsible tool calls, live plans/todos, embedded diffs + terminals, subagent nesting, activity bar, permission UI, thinking blocks) is **already provided** by the shared `AcpThread` / `ThreadView` infrastructure. We only needed the wiring + discoverability.
- `todo!()` is the official mechanism for deferring Mac/Windows work (see below). It is acceptable and encouraged in this feature area.

### Linux implementation (current canonical state)
- Standard install location after `curl -fsSL https://x.ai/cli/install.sh | bash` is `~/.grok/bin/grok`.
- `default_command_for_grok()` (in `agent_server_store.rs`) checks that path + a few common alternatives, then falls back to the bare name "grok" (PATH).
- Because the check is performed at `AgentServerStore` rebuild time, a Linux user who has the binary sees "Grok Build" in the agent selector with a working default **with zero entries in settings.json**.

**Clarified desired default experience (user directive):** For users who select Grok (bridged or native `is_grok_build_profile`), the **fully maximized agent screen** — the `ZedTodosDockPrototype` / `ZedTodosSurface` overlay in Full Agent Mode (big dock with the complete classified surface: approvals with RO/Destructive chips, proposed plans + accept, background monitors with lazy terminals, Grok memory, all sections pre-expanded via `prepare_for_full_agent_mode`, plus zoom) — is intended to be the primary/default view on editor start and Grok activation. The goal is to feel like the immersive standalone Grok Build TUI, not "normal code editor with an agent sidebar." The existing `ensure_grok_categorized_surface` + `open_zed_todos_surface` + `toggle_zoom` + focus machinery is the path to achieve this. Guards for "hang of doom" (heavy Grok init on pure load) should be respected where they protect launch performance, but the maximized agent view should be the default once the context is Grok.
- Explicit user configuration under `agent_servers.grok` always wins (the synthetic entry is only inserted when the key is absent).
- Keybinding added to `assets/keymaps/default-linux.json` (`ctrl-alt-x`).
- Documentation updated in `docs/src/ai/external-agents.md` with exact Linux-friendly example.
- Unit test `test_default_command_for_grok_linux_fallback` guarantees the function never returns `None` on Linux.

### Mac and Windows porting obligations (mandatory reading)
When porting to macOS:
- The install script produces the identical `~/.grok/bin/grok` location, so the existing HOME-based candidates already work. macOS-specific keybinding (`cmd-alt-x`) added to `default-macos.json` (G-08).
- The one Linux-only `#[cfg(target_os = "linux")]` test (candidates order) was generalized to `any(target_os = "linux", target_os = "macos")`; Linux behavior untouched. No other Linux-only guards needed removal for this slice.

When porting to Windows:
- The user may have installed via Git Bash, PowerShell, Scoop, WinGet, or a native Windows build.
- Typical resolved paths: `%USERPROFILE%\.grok\bin\grok.exe`, or a plain `grok` on PATH after the installer updates the environment.
- You **must** replace the `todo!()` inside `default_command_for_grok` (under `#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]`) with real logic.
- Add the equivalent keybinding to `default-windows.json`.
- Add Windows-specific integration tests (use `#[cfg(target_os = "windows")]`).
- Update the onboarding `FEATURED_AGENT_IDS` and any "suggested agents" UI only after the Windows experience is verified.

**Rule:** Never remove a `todo!()` for another platform without also adding the corresponding tests and keymap entry.

### Use of the `todo!()` macro in this feature
We deliberately use the Rust `todo!("reason")` macro (not `unimplemented!` or `panic!`) for any code path that is not yet correct on a non-Linux platform. Examples already present:
- `default_command_for_grok` Windows arm
- Comments in the Linux keymap entry pointing at the other two keymap files

When you hit one of these at runtime on the wrong platform you get a clear message that tells you exactly which section of AGENTS.md to read.

### Test strategy (must be followed for all future changes)
1. Every new public or `pub(crate)` helper (`default_command_for_grok`, any future `grok_command_for_platform`, etc.) gets a unit test that runs on Linux.
2. The deserializer round-trip test in `agent_ui/src/agent_ui.rs` (`test_deserialize_new_external_agent_thread`) must continue to cover the literal string `"grok"`.
3. Any change to icon/logo logic must be exercised by a test that constructs a `CustomAgentServer` for `GROK_ID` and asserts the returned `IconName`.
4. Integration coverage lives in the existing `agent_panel` serialization/restore tests and the `common_e2e_tests!` macro used by `NativeAgentServer` and ACP harnesses. When adding Mac/Windows behavior, extend those.
5. After any edit, **instruct the user** to run **exactly** the following command (and wait for them to paste the output back). Never run it yourself:

   `./script/clippy -p project -p agent_servers -p agent_ui` (never raw `cargo clippy`).
6. Prefer `cx.background_executor().timer(...)` in any new GPUI tests that involve the Grok agent connection (see the "Timers in tests" section at the top of this file).
7. For Grok Build sessions (native `is_grok_build_profile` or bridged "grok" ACP), Zed automatically pushes compact LSP diagnostic counts (errors/warnings from rust-analyzer etc.) into the agent's prompt context on every turn. The model is explicitly instructed to rely on these Zed-sourced lints first instead of external clippy or shell commands. When working as the agent, use the pushed data; only fall back to running clippy when the model itself requests it for a specific reason.

### Grok / ZT-1 Regression Protection (Task 1 during native P4 work)

The Verification & No-Regression Harness agent delivered the following frequently-executable command set (in its final summary) to protect the bridged Grok (ACP) + ZT-1 surface during all future native Grok (P4) work. Any native Grok developer must have the human run these exact commands after every slice/edit (the agent itself must never execute cargo or cargo wrappers — see the CRITICAL PROCESS RULE at the top of this Grok section). This protects against regressions of the kind the harness itself surfaced (e.g. plan-risk test / PlanEntry issues under full features, ZT-1/plan/approval/memory/profile fidelity).

Copy-paste ready (these are commands the **human user** must run after edits — the agent must never execute them itself):

- Precise clippy line (targeted to core Grok/ZT-1 crates covering both bridged and native paths):

  `./script/clippy -p project -p agent -p agent_ui -p acp_thread -p agent_servers`

- Key filtered `cargo test` commands for the ZT-1/grok/plan/approval/memory surface:

  `cargo test -p acp_thread --test acp_thread -- test_approval_risk_classification_on_grok_plan_tools test_plan_phase_explicit_states_and_transitions`

  `cargo test -p project --test agent_server_store -- grok`

  `cargo test -p agent --test native_grok_contracts -- grok`

  (extend filters as needed to cover plan/approval/memory/enter_plan_mode tests in those packages)

- `cargo audit`

- When a display and real `grok` binary is available: run the manual roundtrip scenarios exercising ACP thread + plan/todo/monitor flows, session ID copy/resume via `grok -r`, and memory surface parity checks.

These gates must be part of every P4 native slice to keep Task 1 (bridged Grok + ZT-1) solid.

### Current known limitations / future work (do not regress)
- No dedicated SVG icon yet (we reuse `AiXAi`). A future task is to add `assets/icons/grok.svg` (or `ai_grok.svg`) and register it so the synthesized entry can supply a custom icon path. **Status: Rejected** on efficiency grounds (see Performance subsection above). We will only extend `IconName` with native GPUI-backed variants if truly needed.
- Richer Grok-specific UI (persona badges on subagents, dedicated background-task monitor pane, one-click "approve plan" flow) belongs in Phase 2 of the original implementation plan.
- Bridging of Grok's native skill directories (`.grok/skills`) with Zed's `.agents/skills` completed as G-10 (efficient dual scan, TDD, clippy-clean, perf documented). G-15/G-16 etc remain for deeper co-equal features (e.g. bundled resource parity, sessions).
- Session resume/roundtrip from `~/.grok/sessions/` (TUI IDs) now low-friction via ID copy in grok threads + clipboard plain-ID detection (G-16). Full offline jsonl replay remains gated.

**See GROK_SEAMLESS_FRICTION_MAP.md (created 2026-05-19 by Seamless Integration Friction Auditor) for the complete actionable friction map from the bridging era.** 

**Full Native Replacement Commitment (2026-05-20 — decisive, non-incremental user directive):**  

The "bridging first, native later" approach is rejected. Zed is now fully committed to a **complete, independent native Rust + GPUI re-implementation** that delivers **all** Grok Build capabilities with **no runtime dependency** on the external `grok` binary for normal operation.

The binding requirements (user's exact 1.3 language) are:
1. A non-bridged, fully independent, cross-compatible implementation of Grok Build inside Zed (full sub-agent tracking with personas and capability modes, rich native GPUI visuals instead of TUI, all skills and personas, monitor, plan approval, etc.).
2. Modifications to Zed's ACP and agent layer so that **IDE-based diagnostics** (errors and warnings from rust-analyzer and other language servers) become the primary, always-available, non-blocking, cross-language context for the agent. The agent must be explicitly instructed to rely on Zed-sourced lints first rather than running local `cargo` or shell commands.
3. Zed provides real system (desktop + in-Zed) notifications when all autonomous work finishes (the exact required completion phrase).

The external `grok` binary and `grok agent stdio` are now **legacy compatibility only**. They exist for users who still want to use the standalone TUI, but they are no longer the development target or a required dependency for new Grok Build features in Zed.

All future agents, architecture decisions, and the 16-agent swarm operate under this charter: **ruthlessly native, replace bridges, prefer Zed primitives, IDE diagnostics first-class, full test coverage**.

