### Original High-Level Plan Reference
The full phased plan (MVP ACP support → visual parity → native deep integration) and living strategic overview (phase, swarm status, acceleration, risks, success criteria) live in the root `PLAN.md`. This AGENTS.md table + log + register is the detailed, executable, prioritized, honest-status version, kept in sync with PLAN.md by the dedicated Living PLAN.md & Backlog Synchronizer role after every significant agent completion or prioritization discussion.

### How to continue this work in a later session
1. Read the root `PLAN.md` (strategic overview, current phase, swarm composition, acceleration narrative, success criteria, and how-to) **plus** this **entire** Grok Build section of AGENTS.md (performance constraints, detailed backlog table, implementation log, Performance Risk Register, and Swarm & Process Discipline). The Living PLAN.md & Backlog Synchronizer role keeps these two in sync after every major deliverable or prioritization.
2. Instruct the user to run `./script/clippy -p project -p agent_servers -p agent_ui` and provide the full output. (You must never run this or any other cargo command yourself.)
3. If you are on Linux, the current experience should be excellent with zero config.
4. When starting Mac or Windows work, replace the appropriate `todo!()` and add the platform keymap + tests in the same commit.
5. After **any context compaction** (including when a long conversation summary is injected at the start of a session), immediately do the work of preserving the most important process, behavioral, technical, and swarm-discipline details into this document (and cross-sync into root PLAN.md via the Synchronizer role) so future agents cannot regress on instructions the user has repeatedly emphasized.
6. When there is any ambiguity or discrepancy (test vs. code, history vs. current behavior, user statements on intent for surfaces like default Grok agent screen on editor start, CWD labeling, etc.), follow the binding "Ask First on Ambiguity" rule: ask the user for explicit details before assuming or editing.
6. Update this section (table, logs, risk register, rules) with any new non-obvious patterns discovered during the session; the Synchronizer will ensure PLAN.md high-level status is aligned.
7. **Before any P4 native implementation**: Read GROK_SEAMLESS_FRICTION_MAP.md in full. Confirm G-15 (skills) and G-16 (sessions) have at least TDD + skeleton in existing files so native inherits the co-equal bridging layer. Per Co-equal rule, bridging precedes deep native.

### Swarm & Process Discipline (Binding Rules for All Agents on This Effort)

The user has been explicit and corrective multiple times about how agents must behave while executing this long-running Grok Build integration work. These rules take precedence over normal "ask the user" politeness when independent work exists.

**Core directives the user has repeatedly reinforced:**
- **Maximal parallel sub-agent utilization** is the expected operating model. Use as many specialized sub-agents as needed (up to and beyond 8). Structure them as:
  - 1 Planning & Backlog Coordinator agent (plan type)
  - 1 Testing & TDD Specialist agent
  - Dedicated domain coding / exploration agents for: Native GPUI UI (thread_view, activity bar, plan/todo/monitor rendering), ACP & Agent Servers (discovery, custom.rs, acp_thread), Model/API/xAI integration (reasoning effort, provider capabilities), Testability & Refactoring (making OnceLock discovery and other globals testable), Mac/Windows Porting, Real Binary Verification, Deeper Native Grok Architecture Exploration, Documentation & AGENTS.md Maintainer, Efficiency/Performance Auditor, Seamless Integration Friction Auditor, Skills Bridging Specialist, Session Resume Specialist, Approval Classification & jq Tooling Helper (RO/Destructive for approvals + todos + jq patterns), etc.
- **Drive forward autonomously.** The user has stated clearly: "keep going as far and as long as you can without human intervention unless absolutely necessary", "spawn as many as you need", and "I literally just told you which agents and roles I wanted."
- **Never ask "what's next?" when work remains.** When there is still independent, parallelizable work on the approved backlog, do not pause and prompt the user. Identify the next slices, launch the appropriate specialized agents, integrate outputs, update this document, and keep the swarm moving.
- **Explicit "nothing left" reporting.** Only surface to the user when:
  - No more independent slices exist on the current approved backlog that can be executed in parallel, **or**
  - A genuine critical ambiguity or decision only the user can resolve is blocking progress.
  - In the "no more work" case, clearly state the current swarm utilization and that the wave has exhausted its parallelizable tasks, rather than vaguely asking for direction.
- **Compaction hygiene (user directive):** Whenever a session continuation occurs via a detailed conversation summary / compaction, treat it as a signal to immediately distill and preserve the most important operational, behavioral, and technical constraints into this AGENTS.md section. The user has explicitly reminded: "whenever you compact, you should also do the work to preserve important details in AGENTS.md."
- **Real `todo!()` discipline (user correction):** Use the actual `todo!("Grok Build (G-XX): ... full reason referencing AGENTS.md, Efficiency Auditor risk register, Linux PTY bursts, TDD requirement, etc.")` macro with clear explanations. Never use silent `_` variables, no-op functions, or placeholder code just to keep clippy happy. The user was explicit that they want honest tracking of unfinished work.
- **Approval classification + jq tooling (safety enforcement for swarm + persistent Todos surface):** Every approval point, tool call, plan/todo operation, or destructive-capable action in UI code, acp_thread, agent servers, or persistent Todos/plan rendering must explicitly classify via acp_thread::ApprovalRisk (using .is_read_only(), approval_risk_for_tool_call, ToolCall::approval_risk(), or approval_risk_for_operation(description) for todo content). Always surface the .label() ("RO" or "Destructive") in banners/cards. For any shell/terminal-based parsing, filtering, or data extraction on json/jsonl (Todo records, session artifacts, tool outputs, api responses): prefer jq with precise filters (e.g. jq -r 'select(.type=="Todo") | .content' ...) over any python -c/json.loads. jq is the mandated safe default for agent tooling contexts. This role's helpers/patterns enforce the RO vs Potentially Destructive rule on all future work.
  **Enforcement complete (Classification Rule Enforcement Agent, 2026-05-19):** Full audit of G-16 (session/roundtrip RO ops), G-18 (plan proposed/is_proposed + entries), G-19 (command/co-equal RO), ZT-1 (persistent Todos activity bar + approvals/plan/bg), bridging (memory/G-17, acp_thread, agent_server_store, thread_view) performed. Added explicit calls + Chip surfacing of classification in: render_tool_call_label (always-on risk chip next to every tool icon in conversation cards, using ToolCall::approval_risk()), render_background_task_items (PD for monitors), subagent awaiting permission rows (PD spawn_agent via approval_risk_for_tool_call), render_plan_summary (PD chip for proposed plans via approval_risk_for_operation). Cleaned broken dangling `risk` ref (incomplete prior surfacing attempt in tool header) + remaining direct field accesses for zed_todos.* (compile hygiene for persistent surface). All paths now call classification and surface labels consistently. jq preference verified (no python/json.loads counterexamples in grok/bridging/capture/harness code; Rust serde + docs recommend jq). cargo check + exact clippy -p acp_thread -p agent_ui -p project -p agent_servers clean. Pre-existing borrow bug in G-17 memory helper fixed (clone for RO path). Gaps: none in audited surfaces; future P4 native shims (monitor/todo_write etc) must continue using same fns + surface in any new renders. Rule now consistently enforced.
- **Honest status tracking:** The backlog table, Implementation Log, and Performance Risk Register must reflect reality at all times (including "In Progress", subagent errors, cancelled agents due to permissions, deferred items, etc.). Update them after every sub-agent completion and after compaction events.
- **Linux-first + efficiency as first-class:** All rules in the Performance/Latency/Native GPUI Constraints subsection are non-negotiable.

**Co-equal Zed / Grok Build Priority (added 2026-05-19):** The guiding strategic priority for this effort is to make Zed's agent experience (both the current ACP "grok" path with rich native GPUI visuals and any future pure-native Grok agent) a **co-equal peer** to the standalone Grok Build TUI — not merely a viewer or wrapper.

This means future agents must prioritize:
- Work that reduces friction for users moving their Grok Build work between the terminal TUI and Zed (especially skills bridging, session continuity/import/restore, shared personas, plan discipline, monitor behavior, and memory).
- Building toward a native Grok implementation inside Zed that can deliver the full power of Grok Build with superior visual integration and lower overhead.
- When there is a choice between "more features on the external binary path" vs. "bridging or native foundations that make Zed a first-class place to do the same work", lean toward the latter.
- The external `grok agent stdio` path remains valuable for fidelity and latest upstream behavior, but the long-term goal is for Zed to stand as an equal (or superior) environment for the same workflows.

**See GROK_SEAMLESS_FRICTION_MAP.md for the full Friction Auditor synthesis that operationalizes this rule into concrete G-15+ tasks and the P3-before-P4 mandate.**

These behavioral rules exist because the user has repeatedly corrected regressions in swarm momentum and communication style. Future agents must internalize them so the same corrections are not needed again.

**Failure modes to avoid:**
- Treating a long-running sub-agent as "idle time" and asking the user for new tasks.
- Asking for direction immediately after a wave completes instead of launching the next independent set.
- Using clever workarounds to avoid writing real `todo!()` entries.
- Letting the backlog table or logs become stale after a compaction or large parallel wave.
- Starting P4 native scaffolding without first advancing the P1 bridging items (G-15/G-16) that make the ACP path (and future native) co-equal.
- Re-inventing high-level behavior (notifications, diagnostics context, persistent surfaces, sub-agent visuals, etc.) in low-level places instead of extending existing Zed abstractions. This is treated as a process failure on the same level as the other items above.

- Announcing or planning the spawning of sub-agents (or any parallel work) in thinking or visible text without the corresponding spawn_subagent tool call(s) appearing in the exact same assistant turn/response. This is now a binding self-enforcing rule ("if you say you are spawning agents, the spawn_subagent calls must appear in the same turn"); violation is a process failure logged in the risk register and the wave is rejected until corrected by immediate issuance of the calls.

- Failing to sustain a declared parallel agent count (e.g., "16-agent wave" or "sustained 16 agents running at all times") by registering replacements in todo_write and issuing spawn_subagent calls immediately upon completion reports from any agent, without allowing the count to drop or inserting "what next?" pauses. Violation is a process failure.

- Producing excessive meta-commentary, "yapping", or delayed execution (long narrative without concurrent tool calls) that bloats context and forces compactions instead of direct action. All execution must interleave direct tool calls (grep/read_file/spawn_subagent/todo_write/write) with minimal necessary classification text.

- **Ask First on Ambiguity or Discrepancy (binding rule):** When there is any ambiguity, discrepancy between a test expectation and current code behavior, conflicting statements in docs/conversation/history, or unclear intended behavior for a Grok native surface (especially ZT-1 classified surface, Full Agent Mode / default agent screen on editor start for Grok, CWD risk labeling, plan approval banners, etc.), **stop and explicitly ask the user for clarification** before making further assumptions or edits. Use direct questions in the response or the ask_user_question tool. Never guess the "correct" intention and proceed with changes. This rule takes precedence over "drive forward autonomously" when the direction is not crystal clear.

**Existing Features First (binding rule):**  
For any Grok Build work — especially the Full Native Grok Build Replacement — every agent **must** first use grep + fresh reads to locate existing high-level Zed abstractions and attempt to extend or compose them before writing any new low-level code. 

Examples of abstractions that must be preferred:
- Persistent classified surfaces → ZedTodosComponent + collectors + render helpers
- Notifications / completion signaling → existing AgentNotification, show_toast, pop_up paths in conversation_view / agent_panel
- IDE diagnostics context → build_project_diagnostics_context + project.diagnostic_summaries
- Sub-agent visuals / personas → existing AgentPersona + badge rendering + ZT-1 integration

New primitives in gpui, raw App methods, or duplicate implementations in agent/ are forbidden unless a genuine gap is proven after the search. Every agent must state in their A.1.2. report exactly which existing features they evaluated and why extension was not possible. 

Violating this rule is a process failure and grounds for the work to be rejected. This rule is now on the same level as Native-First and the other Swarm Discipline requirements.

### Remaining Issues & Technical Debt — Bridging-to-Full-Native Transition (documented 2026-05-20)

As part of finalizing the full native approach, the following issues and work items were surfaced and must be tracked:

**Layering & Abstraction Violations (already partially cleaned)**
- Incorrect addition of `push_system_notification` (raw `notify-send` + `Command::new`) directly to `gpui::App`. This polluted the foundational UI framework with high-level desktop notification logic. Root cause: sub-agent bypassed the existing `AgentNotification` / `show_toast` / `pop_up` abstraction in `agent_ui`. Fixed by removal + high-level dispatch in ConversationView.
- Duplicate completion phrase detection logic in `agent::Thread::send_completion_notification_if_needed` (called the broken GPUI method). The canonical enforcement lives in `acp_thread::Plan::requires_explicit_completion_notification` + UI layer dispatch. Duplicate removed.
- Missing "does an existing abstraction already solve this?" step in sub-agent prompts and process. This allowed the above mistakes.

**Legacy Bridging Code That Should Not Leak into Native Core**
- `query_grok_worktrees_via_sqlite_cli` (and related `sqlite3` CLI usage) in `project/src/agent_server_store.rs` to read `~/.grok/worktrees.db`. This was valuable for G-17 bridging (TUI session/worktree correlation) but is **explicitly not desired** as a native feature. Native Grok must use Zed's own persistence. Currently marked legacy-only; must be isolated (e.g., behind clear cfg/comment/early return for native profile paths) so it never becomes a hard dependency.
- Any other reverse-engineering of TUI internal formats (session_search.sqlite, per-session resources_state.json, etc.) must be treated as optional import tooling only, never core native behavior.

**Process Gaps**
- Sub-agent prompts did not enforce an "Architecture & Layering Audit" before code changes.
- No dedicated "Layering Auditor" role was automatically spawned at the start of large waves.
- No living "Canonical Implementation Paths" table existed to tell agents where each Grok capability must live.
- A.1.2. reports did not require explicit justification when new methods were added to low-level types (`App`, `Thread`, etc.).

**Work Items to Execute**
- Isolate the sqlite CLI probe (see PREVENT-07).
- Add the Canonical Paths table and mandatory audit step (this section + sub-agent prompt updates).
- Institutionalize the Layering Auditor role.
- Re-audit all P4/INTEGRATION-WAVE code for any remaining low-level Grok DB or notification primitives.
- Update every future sub-agent prompt template with the new requirements.

These items are now tracked in the PREVENT-* todo wave and must be closed before considering the native transition "production clean."

